# An in-memory LaTeX book

This chapter owns `src/publication-latex.ts`. It consumes the controlled Pandoc
bridge, not a second Markdown parser. Root SETTINGS use the shared resolver;
no files, processes, browser APIs, or publishing commands are involved. The injected converter is trusted:
it must convert JSON to a LaTeX **body**, preserve raw LaTeX, use top-level chapters,
and disable Pandoc syntax highlighting (`--no-highlight`, tested on Pandoc 3.7.0.2).
The options passed to it make that contract explicit; they are not CLI arguments.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX.*>>= ./src/publication-latex.ts $
<<publication LaTeX imports>>
<<publication LaTeX API>>
<<publication LaTeX options>>
<<publication LaTeX encoding>>
<<publication LaTeX template>>
<<publication LaTeX code>>
<<publication LaTeX contributions>>
<<publication LaTeX render>>
```

Highlighting is a separate full-context adapter. Its UTF-16 spans address display
text with escaped literal openers already unescaped, and actual references still
spelled with their original delimiters. Exceptions pass through the bridge gate.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX imports>>=
import type { ParsedProject, PublicationContribution, GenerationDiagnostic } from './engine';
import { preparePandoc } from './publication-pandoc';
import { resolvePublicationSettings } from './publication-settings';
import { checkFontFace, fontFaces, fontFile, PortableFont } from './publication-fonts';
import type { PublicationSettingsResult } from './publication-settings';
import type { PandocDocument, PandocBlock } from './publication-pandoc';
import { defaultThemeName, getTheme } from './themes';
import type { LiterateTheme } from './themes';
import { highlightContribution, syntaxStyle } from './publication-highlight';
```

The callback must not run after a preparation error. Results are atomic: diagnostics
never accompany partial LaTeX. Installed-font lookup and PDF compilation are caller work.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX API>>=
export interface LatexOptions {
    theme?: string;
    paper?: 'A4' | 'A5' | 'letter' | 'legal';
    marginMm?: number;
    marginTopMm?: number;
    marginRightMm?: number;
    marginBottomMm?: number;
    marginLeftMm?: number;
    bodyFont?: string;
    headingFont?: string;
    codeFont?: string;
    fragmentFont?: string;
    noteFont?: string;
    codeLigatures?: boolean;
    portableFonts?: Record<string, PortableFont>;
}
export interface LatexConversionOptions {
    standalone: false;
    syntaxHighlighting: 'none';
    topLevelDivision: 'chapter';
}
export type LatexConverter = (document: PandocDocument,
    options: LatexConversionOptions) => Promise<string>;
export interface LatexResult { latex?: string; diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[]; }
interface CheckedOptions {
    theme: LiterateTheme; paper: string; geometry: string;
    bodyFont: string; headingFont: string; codeFont: string;
    fragmentFont: string; noteFont: string; codeLigatures: boolean;
    portableFonts?: Record<string, PortableFont>;
}
```

Only catalogue palettes are accepted, with no print remapping. Each side falls
back to `marginMm`, then 20 mm. All supplied margins must be finite in [5, 50],
leaving at least 80 mm width and 100 mm height after subtracting opposite sides.
Family names deliberately exclude fontspec options, paths, and TeX metacharacters.

Root settings resolve first, including note inheritance from the root body.
Per-role programmatic overrides apply next: `bodyFont` does not retroactively
change the resolved note family; use `noteFont` to override that role. This avoids
mistaking an explicit root note equal to its body for an inherited value.
Invalid root settings remain errors even when an option overrides that role.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX options>>=
function fontName(value: string): string {
    if (typeof value !== 'string' || !/^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9 .-]{0,99}$/.test(value)) {
        throw new Error('Font must be a simple family name (1–100 ASCII characters)');
    }
    return value;
}
function checkOptions(options: LatexOptions, settings: PublicationSettingsResult): CheckedOptions {
    const theme = getTheme(options.theme ?? defaultThemeName);
    if (!theme) { throw new Error('Unknown publication theme'); }
    const papers = new Map<string, [string, number, number]>([
        ['A4', ['a4paper', 210, 297]], ['A5', ['a5paper', 148, 210]],
        ['letter', ['letterpaper', 215.9, 279.4]], ['legal', ['legalpaper', 215.9, 355.6]]
    ]);
    const paper = papers.get(options.paper ?? 'A4');
    const margin = options.marginMm ?? 20;
    const overrides = [options.marginTopMm, options.marginRightMm,
        options.marginBottomMm, options.marginLeftMm];
    const [top, right, bottom, left] = overrides.map(value => value ?? margin);
    if (!paper || [margin, top, right, bottom, left].some(value =>
        !Number.isFinite(value) || value < 5 || value > 50) ||
        paper[1] - left - right < 80 || paper[2] - top - bottom < 100) {
        throw new Error('Invalid paper or margin; require 5–50 mm and an 80 × 100 mm body');
    }
    const geometry = overrides.some(value => value !== undefined)
        ? `top=${top}mm,right=${right}mm,bottom=${bottom}mm,left=${left}mm`
        : `margin=${margin}mm`;
```

The boolean policy is checked at runtime too; callers need not be TypeScript.
Defaults come only from the shared settings resolver, not a second catalogue.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX options>>=+
    const codeLigatures = options.codeLigatures ?? settings.codeLigatures;
    if (typeof codeLigatures !== 'boolean') { throw new Error('Code ligatures must be boolean'); }
    return { theme, paper: paper[0], geometry, codeLigatures, portableFonts: options.portableFonts,
        bodyFont: fontName(options.bodyFont ?? settings.fonts.body),
        headingFont: fontName(options.headingFont ?? settings.fonts.heading),
        codeFont: fontName(options.codeFont ?? settings.fonts.code),
        fragmentFont: fontName(options.fragmentFont ?? settings.fonts.fragment),
        noteFont: fontName(options.noteFont ?? settings.fonts.note) };
}
```

Data is never interpolated as commands. Destination identifiers are encoded again
in a private namespace so even a manually constructed snapshot cannot inject TeX.
Name wrapping inserts discretionary breaks, not per-character font groups. We cap
names at 512 Unicode code points; larger names fail explicitly, never abbreviate.
Control characters are rejected rather than interpreted by TeX.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX encoding>>=
function checkText(text: string): void {
    if (/[\u0000-\u0008\u000b-\u001f\u007f]/u.test(text)) {
        throw new Error('Unsupported control character in publication text');
    }
}
function tex(text: string): string {
    checkText(text);
    const escapes = new Map([
        ['\\', '\\textbackslash{}'], ['{', '\\{'], ['}', '\\}'], ['#', '\\#'],
        ['$', '\\$'], ['%', '\\%'], ['&', '\\&'], ['_', '\\_'],
        ['^', '\\textasciicircum{}'], ['~', '\\textasciitilde{}']
    ]);
    return text.replace(/[\\{}#$%&_^~]/g, character => escapes.get(character)!);
}
function target(value: string): string {
    let result = 'lit-';
    for (let index = 0; index < value.length; index++) {
        result += value.charCodeAt(index).toString(16).padStart(4, '0');
    }
    return result;
}
```

Display names use independent break opportunities without altering destinations.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX encoding>>=+
function nameText(name: string): string {
    if (Array.from(name).length > 512 || /[\r\n\t]/.test(name)) {
        throw new Error('Fragment names must be single-line and at most 512 code points');
    }
    return Array.from(name, character => tex(character)).join('\\allowbreak{}');
}
```

Colours are validated even though the catalogue and style adapter are trusted.
The code font retains common/contextual ligatures by default; disabling them
adds `RawFeature={-calt}` to disable contextual substitutions. Lilex and ordinary
family overrides use their native italic faces. Only the exact legacy
D2CodingLigature Nerd Font Propo family retains automatic slant in family-name
mode, since it has no italic files. Manifest mode always requires explicit faces
or an explicit per-face slant request.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX encoding>>=+
function color(value: string): string {
    if (!/^#[0-9a-fA-F]{6}$/.test(value)) { throw new Error('Invalid syntax/theme colour'); }
    return value.slice(1);
}
function pageLink(id: string, label: string): string {
    const destination = target(id);
    return `\\hyperref[${destination}]{${label}\\textsuperscript{\\notefont\\pageref*{${destination}}}}`;
}
function anchor(id: string): string { return `\\phantomsection\\label{${target(id)}}`; }
```

The book is borderless and the entire page uses the selected background. Ordinary
code uses the renderer's escaped `Verbatim` blocks. The `verbatim` and `Highlighting`
environments remain compatibility aliases, not support for Skylighting token commands. Strikeout and tight lists are
supported. The converter must not emit a standalone preamble or highlighted prose
code. LuaLaTeX is the intended compiler, with installed fonts and packages.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX template>>=
<<publication LaTeX font command>>
<<publication LaTeX companion path>>
<<publication LaTeX portable font command>>
<<publication LaTeX slant features>>
```

Family-name mode lets fontspec resolve native styles for Lilex and other ordinary
families; only the known legacy D2 family keeps its prior synthetic declaration.
Portable mode has no installed-family fallback and no automatic synthesis: only
a manifest's explicit italic-face object can request FakeSlant.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX font command>>=
function fontCommand(command: string, family: string, options: CheckedOptions, code = false): string {
    const features = code ? `Ligatures=Common,Contextuals=Alternate,${options.codeLigatures ? '' : 'RawFeature={-calt},'}` : '';
    if (options.portableFonts !== undefined) {
        const font = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(options.portableFonts, family)
            ? options.portableFonts[family] : undefined;
        if (!font) { throw new Error(`Invalid or missing portable font companions for ${family}`); }
        return portableFontCommand(command, family, font, features);
    }
    if (!code) { return `${command}{${family}}`; }
    if (family !== 'D2CodingLigature Nerd Font Propo') {
        return `${command}[${features.slice(0, -1)}]{${family}}`;
    }
    return `${command}[${features}\nItalicFont={${family}},ItalicFeatures={FakeSlant=0.2},\nBoldItalicFont={${family}},BoldItalicFeatures={FakeSlant=0.2,FakeBold=1.5}]{${family}}`;
}
```

The renderer validates companion names even for programmatic callers. Source
paths and arbitrary TeX options cannot cross this boundary.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX companion path>>=
function companionPath(value: unknown, where: string): string {
    if (typeof value !== 'string' ||
        !/^lit-fonts-[a-f0-9]{64}\/[a-f0-9]{64}\.(ttf|otf)$/.test(value)) {
        throw new Error(`Invalid or missing portable font companions for ${where}`);
    }
    return value;
}
```

Fontspec receives a separate Path and basename-only face names: slashes in its
main font argument would otherwise leak into LaTeX's internal family identifier.
Shared validation checks synthesis fields before any face is interpolated.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX portable font command>>=
function portableFontCommand(command: string, family: string, font: PortableFont, features: string): string {
    const checked = {} as PortableFont;
    for (const face of fontFaces) {
        checked[face] = checkFontFace(font[face], face, `${family}.${face}`, companionPath);
    }
    const regular = fontFile(checked.regular);
    const directory = regular.slice(0, regular.lastIndexOf('/') + 1);
    if (fontFaces.some(face => !fontFile(checked[face]).startsWith(directory))) {
        throw new Error(`Portable font faces must share one companion directory for ${family}`);
    }
    const name = (face: keyof PortableFont): string => fontFile(checked[face]).slice(directory.length);
    return `${command}[${features}Path={${directory}},BoldFont={${name('bold')}},` +
        `ItalicFont={${name('italic')}},BoldItalicFont={${name('boldItalic')}}` +
        `${slantFeatures(checked)}]{${name('regular')}}`;
}
```

Only explicitly synthesized faces receive a feature. Plain string mappings remain
strict file mappings, and a boldItalic synthesis uses its supplied bold file rather
than silently applying FakeBold. The bounded numbers stringify as TeX decimals.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX slant features>>=
function slantFeatures(font: PortableFont): string {
    let features = '';
    for (const [face, option] of [['italic', 'ItalicFeatures'], ['boldItalic', 'BoldItalicFeatures']] as const) {
        const choice = font[face];
        if (typeof choice !== 'string') { features += `,${option}={FakeSlant=${choice.slant}}`; }
    }
    return features;
}
```

```ts : <<publication LaTeX template>>=+
function template(body: string, options: CheckedOptions, definitions: string[]): string {
    const c = options.theme.colors;
    return themeNotice() + String.raw`\documentclass[11pt,oneside,openany]{scrbook}
${'\\usepackage'}{fontspec}
${fontCommand('\\setmainfont', options.bodyFont, options)}
${fontCommand('\\newfontfamily\\headingfont', options.headingFont, options)}
${fontCommand('\\newfontfamily\\fragmentfont', options.fragmentFont, options)}
${fontCommand('\\newfontfamily\\notefont', options.noteFont, options)}
${fontCommand('\\setmonofont', options.codeFont, options, true)}
${'\\usepackage'}[${options.paper},${options.geometry}]{geometry}
${'\\usepackage'}{fvextra,needspace,xcolor,microtype,amssymb,amsmath}
${'\\usepackage'}[normalem]{ulem}
${'\\usepackage'}[hidelinks]{hyperref}
\definecolor{publicationBackground}{HTML}{${color(c.background)}}
\definecolor{publicationText}{HTML}{${color(c.text)}}
\definecolor{publicationAccent}{HTML}{${color(c.accent)}}
\pagecolor{publicationBackground}\color{publicationText}
\setkomafont{disposition}{\headingfont\bfseries\color{publicationAccent}}
\setkomafont{pageheadfoot}{\notefont\color{publicationText}}
\setkomafont{footnote}{\notefont}
\providecommand{\tightlist}{\setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}\setlength{\parskip}{0pt}}
\providecommand{\passthrough}[1]{#1}
\providecommand{\pandocbounded}[1]{#1}
` + templateCodeSettings() + '\n' + definitions.join('\n') +
        '\n\\begin{document}\n\\tableofcontents\n' + body + '\n\\end{document}\n';
}
```

`FancyVerbBreakStart/Stop` lets fvextra inspect the contents of a styled run.
Styles group runs rather than glyphs. This retains shaping within a run; a style
boundary can still split a ligature. PDF copying is not a source-code transport.
The renderer harness validates styled-run wrapping and contextual glyph substitution
on three A5 palettes. Ligatures ON deliberately changes operator PDF extraction;
this is not an exact-copy guarantee, even when long plain tokens reconstruct.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX template>>=+
function templateCodeSettings(): string {
    return String.raw`\fvset{fontsize=\small,breaklines=true,breakanywhere=true,
breakautoindent=false,breaksymbolleft={},breaksymbolright={},
breakanywheresymbolpre={},breakanywheresymbolpost={},frame=none}
\providecommand{\st}[1]{\sout{#1}}
\DefineVerbatimEnvironment{Highlighting}{Verbatim}{}
\RecustomVerbatimEnvironment{verbatim}{Verbatim}{}
\newcommand{\LitStyle}[4]{{\color[HTML]{#1}#2#3\FancyVerbBreakStart#4\FancyVerbBreakStop}}
\setlength{\parindent}{0pt}
\setlength{\parskip}{5pt}
\pagestyle{headings}`;
}
```

Only the three command characters need escaping inside Verbatim. Other TeX
metacharacters keep verbatim catcodes. Escaping the backslash also prevents a
literal end-environment string from ending the environment. Each style run is
split at physical newlines so no macro argument crosses a Verbatim line.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX code>>=
type Highlighted = ReturnType<typeof highlightContribution>;
function verbatimText(text: string): string {
    checkText(text);
    return text.replace(/[\\{}]/g, character => {
        if (character === '\\') { return '\\textbackslash{}'; }
        return character === '{' ? '\\textbraceleft{}' : '\\textbraceright{}';
    });
}
function styled(text: string, scope: string, theme: LiterateTheme): string {
    const style = syntaxStyle(scope, theme);
    return text.split('\n').map(line => line.length ?
        `\\LitStyle{${color(style.color)}}{${style.bold ? '\\bfseries' : ''}}` +
        `{${style.italic ? '\\itshape' : ''}}{${verbatimText(line)}}` : '').join('\n');
}
```

Literal slices retain whitespace and intersect full-context style ranges.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX code>>=+
function literal(data: Highlighted, start: number, end: number, theme: LiterateTheme): string {
    if (start === end) { return ''; }
    let cursor = start;
    let result = '';
    for (const span of data.spans) {
        const left = Math.max(start, span.start), right = Math.min(end, span.end);
        if (left >= right) { continue; }
        result += verbatimText(data.text.slice(cursor, left));
        result += styled(data.text.slice(left, right), span.scope, theme);
        cursor = right;
    }
    result += verbatimText(data.text.slice(cursor, end));
    return '\\begin{Verbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n' + result +
        (result.endsWith('\n') ? '' : '\n') + '\\end{Verbatim}\n';
}
```

The adapter must return ordered, disjoint ranges within the UTF-16 string. Reject
bad ranges rather than silently dropping or repeating source. References take
precedence over syntax styles and preserve the order of all surrounding literals.
Every reference gets a display-only line break and a modest hanging indent. Its
macro is argument-free, defined outside Verbatim, and invoked outside it as well.
The unboxed paragraph can break across pages, unlike the probe's parbox.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX code>>=+
function validateHighlight(data: Highlighted): void {
    checkText(data.text);
    for (const ranges of [data.spans, data.references]) {
        let end = 0;
        for (const range of ranges) {
            if (!Number.isInteger(range.start) || !Number.isInteger(range.end) ||
                range.start < end || range.end <= range.start || range.end > data.text.length) {
                throw new Error('Invalid highlighting ranges');
            }
            end = range.end;
        }
    }
}
function macroName(index: number): string {
    let suffix = '';
    do { suffix = String.fromCharCode(65 + index % 26) + suffix;
        index = Math.floor(index / 26) - 1;
    } while (index >= 0);
    return `LitReference${suffix}`;
}
```

Adjacent spans with identical visual styles must form one run even if highlight.js
split them into different unstyled nodes. This keeps operator shaping independent
of emitter tree boundaries.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX code>>=+
function mergeStyles(data: Highlighted, theme: LiterateTheme): void {
    const merged: Highlighted['spans'] = [];
    let previousStyle = '';
    for (const span of data.spans) {
        const style = JSON.stringify(syntaxStyle(span.scope, theme));
        const previous = merged[merged.length - 1];
        if (previous && previous.end === span.start && style === previousStyle) {
            previous.end = span.end;
        } else { merged.push({ ...span }); }
        previousStyle = style;
    }
    data.spans = merged;
}
```

Family destinations live on base definitions; each contribution also has its own
label. Previous/next links point at contribution pages, not merely the family.
The heading requests room for its start and code, but no entire code block is boxed.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX contributions>>=
function contribution(part: PublicationContribution, project: ParsedProject,
    theme: LiterateTheme, definitions: string[]): PandocBlock[] {
    const family = project.publication.fragments.find(item => item.target === part.fragmentTarget);
    if (!family) { throw new Error('Contribution has no fragment family'); }
    const position = family.contributions.indexOf(part.target);
    if (position < 0) { throw new Error('Contribution is absent from its family'); }
    const data = highlightContribution(part);
    validateHighlight(data);
    mergeStyles(data, theme);
    let output = '\\par\\Needspace{5\\baselineskip}\n' + anchor(part.target);
    if (part.kind === 'definition') { output += anchor(family.target); }
    output += `{\\fragmentfont\\raggedright\\color{publicationAccent} ${nameText(family.name)}` +
        `\\enspace\\ensuremath{${part.kind === 'definition' ? '\\equiv' : '+\\equiv'}}\\par}\n`;
    const navigation: string[] = [];
    if (position > 0) { navigation.push(pageLink(family.contributions[position - 1], 'Previous ')); }
    if (part.kind === 'append') { navigation.push(pageLink(family.target, 'Base ')); }
    if (position + 1 < family.contributions.length) {
        navigation.push(pageLink(family.contributions[position + 1], 'Next '));
    }
    if (navigation.length) { output += `{\\notefont\\small ${navigation.join('\\quad ')}\\par}\n`; }
    output += contributionBody(data, project, theme, definitions);
    return [{ t: 'RawBlock', c: ['latex', output] }];
}
```

Reference names remain complete, with superscript family-definition pages. The
highlighter cannot invent a destination: returned names/targets are checked against
the publication families. The existing bridge independently validates source
references, missing targets, modifiers, and cycles before calling this renderer.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX contributions>>=+
function contributionBody(data: Highlighted, project: ParsedProject,
    theme: LiterateTheme, definitions: string[]): string {
    let cursor = 0, output = '';
    for (const reference of data.references) {
        if (!project.publication.fragments.some(family =>
            family.target === reference.target && family.name === reference.name)) {
            throw new Error('Unknown highlighted reference destination');
        }
        output += literal(data, cursor, referencePrefixEnd(data.text, cursor, reference.start), theme);
        const command = macroName(definitions.length);
        const label = '\\textless{}\\textless{}' + nameText(reference.name) +
            '\\textgreater{}\\textgreater{}';
        definitions.push(`\\newcommand{\\${command}}{\\par\\begingroup` +
            '\\leftskip=1.5em\\rightskip=0pt plus 1fil\\parindent=0pt' +
            '\\fragmentfont\\small\\noindent ' + pageLink(reference.target, label) +
            '\\par\\endgroup}');
        output += `\\${command}{}\n`;
        cursor = reference.end + (data.text[reference.end] === '\n' ? 1 : 0);
    }
    return output + literal(data, cursor, data.text.length, theme);
}
```

The reference paragraph supplies its own terminating line break. Consume exactly
one immediately following newline, leaving any authored blank lines intact. On a
standalone reference line, discard only its indentation, not earlier blank lines;
the reference still uses the common 1.5em hanging display indent.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX contributions>>=+
function referencePrefixEnd(text: string, cursor: number, start: number): number {
    const lineStart = text.lastIndexOf('\n', start - 1) + 1;
    return /^[ \t]*$/.test(text.slice(lineStart, start)) ? Math.max(cursor, lineStart) : start;
}
```

Ordinary fenced and indented code is literal data, never fragment syntax. Render
it ourselves before conversion: even with highlighting disabled, Pandoc switches
to unsupported highlighting macros when code contains a verbatim terminator.
The walker retains prose, inline code and their containers. No opener unescaping
or semantic reference/highlight adapter runs here. Document destinations need one
LaTeX-specific adjustment: Pandoc places a Div anchor before its opening chapter,
so the chapter's page break can strand a whole-document link on the preceding
page. Move that anchor just after an opening heading, retaining both stable IDs.
Empty documents and documents starting with prose keep their original Div anchor.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX code>>=+
function documentAnchor(block: Extract<PandocBlock, { t: 'Div' }>, theme: LiterateTheme): PandocBlock {
    const [attributes, contents] = block.c;
    const children = ordinaryCode(contents, theme);
    if (/^d-(?:[0-9a-f]{4})+$/.test(attributes[0]) && children[0]?.t === 'Header') {
        children.splice(1, 0, { t: 'RawBlock',
            c: ['latex', `\\phantomsection\\label{${attributes[0]}}`] });
        return { t: 'Div', c: [['', attributes[1], attributes[2]], children] };
    }
    return { t: 'Div', c: [attributes, children] };
}
```

```ts : <<publication LaTeX code>>=+
function ordinaryCode(blocks: PandocBlock[], theme: LiterateTheme): PandocBlock[] {
    return blocks.map(block => {
        switch (block.t) {
            case 'CodeBlock': {
                const text = block.c[1];
                const data: Highlighted = { text, references: [],
                    spans: text ? [{ start: 0, end: text.length, scope: '' }] : [] };
                return { t: 'RawBlock', c: ['latex', literal(data, 0, text.length, theme)] };
            }
            case 'Div': return documentAnchor(block, theme);
            case 'BlockQuote': return { ...block, c: ordinaryCode(block.c, theme) };
            case 'BulletList': return { ...block, c: block.c.map(item => ordinaryCode(item, theme)) };
            case 'OrderedList': return { ...block,
                c: [block.c[0], block.c[1].map(item => ordinaryCode(item, theme))] };
            default: return block;
        }
    });
}
```

Only a successful bridge result reaches the injected converter. Converter rejection,
invalid output, or template failure produces a diagnostic and no `latex` property.
The first document provides a stable location for non-source-specific failures.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX render>>=
export async function renderLatex(parsed: ParsedProject, options: LatexOptions,
    convert: LatexConverter): Promise<LatexResult> {
    let stage = 'latex-options';
    try {
        const settings = resolvePublicationSettings(parsed);
        if (settings.diagnostics.length) {
            return { diagnostics: [...parsed.diagnostics, ...settings.diagnostics] };
        }
        const checked = checkOptions(options, settings);
        const definitions: string[] = [];
        stage = 'latex-preparation';
        const prepared = preparePandoc(parsed, part =>
            contribution(part, parsed, checked.theme, definitions));
        if (!prepared.document) { return { diagnostics: prepared.diagnostics }; }
        prepared.document.blocks = ordinaryCode(prepared.document.blocks, checked.theme);
        stage = 'latex-conversion';
        const body = await convert(prepared.document, {
            standalone: false, syntaxHighlighting: 'none', topLevelDivision: 'chapter'
        });
        if (typeof body !== 'string') { throw new Error('Converter must return a LaTeX body string'); }
        stage = 'latex-template';
        return { latex: template(body, checked, definitions), diagnostics: [] };
    } catch (error) {
        return { diagnostics: [{ path: parsed.documents[0]?.path ?? '', line: 0, column: 0,
            code: stage, message: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error) }] };
    }
}
```

## Theme redistribution notice

The palette notice is retained in every emitted TeX document without filesystem
access. This bounded duplication can move to a shared themes export later.

```ts : <<publication LaTeX template>>=+
function themeNotice(): string {
    return [
        'Theme palette snapshot: HErmonica drawing defaults (hermonica),',
        'and NvChad/base46 v2.5 themes; see THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.',
        'nvim-base16.lua is a library for modifying themes with Lua',
        'Copyright © 2019 Ashkan Kiani',
        '',
        'Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining',
        'a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"),',
        'to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation',
        'the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense,',
        'and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the',
        'Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:',
        'The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included',
        'in all copies or substantial portions of the Software.',
        'THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND,',
        'EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES',
        'OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT.',
        'IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM,',
        'DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT,',
        'TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE',
        'OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.'
    ].map(line => '% ' + line).join('\n') + '\n';
}
```

## Deliberate boundaries

Images, raw author markup, tables, Mermaid, and unsupported links still fail in
`preparePandoc`; this is not yet a whole-project book exporter. No assets are
copied, fonts downloaded, or TeX files compiled. The caller must install Latin
Modern, Monofur Nerd Font and Lilex Nerd Font (or supply safe family overrides or
portable companions), and the listed TeX packages. Missing glyphs remain compiler
errors/warnings to check, not a claim of universal Unicode coverage.

Prose and inline-code escaping remain the trusted Pandoc writer's responsibility;
ordinary block code is escaped by this renderer before conversion. Converters must
preserve trusted RawBlocks, without filters, external includes, or highlighting. The ignored `out/publication-renderer` harness
compiles rosepine-dawn, hermonica and flexoki-light A5 fixtures, including nested
containers, full references and long styled tokens. It retains logs, glyph traces,
link/bounds checks and previews. Headless tests cover every catalogue theme, but
all palettes, fonts, arbitrary container depths and pagination are not PDF-validated.
References introduce display-only line breaks; executable copying must use original
or tangled source. CLI adapters, heading identity, packaging,
transactions and generation belong to their separate integration slices.
